    "turn_text": "Talking to them, I'm still involved with the extent I have time to do it. Tree farming, it's my therapy for my new job. I can tell you that. Row crop farming is, I'm still involved because I live on a farm and have relationships with seven or eight farmers on a farm ground. And they tell me they're spending more time fixing ditches, replacing culverts. having to broaden, you know, drainage ways, riparian buffers. And they'll do a lot of that naturally through conservation plans that are already in place. So this came together in a bipartisan way. I'm one of the more fiscally conservative senators out here. There are a ton of us to begin with, so that's not saying much. It's a pretty small group. Many don't pay much attention to that side of the ledger. How do you pay for things? Where I'm from, that's always been something that we've considered along with anything we want to do, all came together here because it was matching pre-existing markets that want to offset their carbon footprint, now with small landowners, tree and egg, where that was mostly just a domain of big companies that had the critical mass to do it.",
